{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792309940,"nonce":"EsKU0OjhE1EAR7ZrNQeLAsK29Gg=","title":"For re-places its children on every reconcile, even when they are unchanged: CodeEditor scroll lags","message":"Reported by the RayDE session, measured on ashui 3d9c201 with RayDE's scripts/scroll-bench.sh (tests/ScrollBench.hx: offscreen, a 4800-line file, scrolled by Scroll.scrollTo, each frame timed by phase). The user reports a laggy code editor, worst while a flick decelerates.\n\n**What happens**\n\n`visibleRows()` reads scroll.y, so it runs again on every offset change and returns a new array. `For`'s Watch never says \"unchanged\", so it reconciles each time, and each reconcile ends in `replaceChildren`. NativeCascade.restyle then calls `blinc_css_children_changed` and `blinc_css_moved` for every sibling; in an interpreter-mode sampler run it was 23% of samples. The editor has two `For` loops, token rows and gutter rows, so it happens twice per step.\n\n**Measured**\n\nFlush per frame by scroll step: 52 px 9.9 ms, 11 px 6.8 ms, 2.4 px 5.9 ms, 0.2 px 5.1 ms, 0 px 0 ms: the cost does not depend on the step. A plain overflow-auto div of 1000 rows of 8 text elements costs 0 ms per step, so scrolling is cheap and the cost is in the editor's reactive graph. With the fix below, a 0.2 px step costs 0.1 ms of flush and the whole frame's median goes from 14.8 to 5.1 ms (editor alone); a flick's deceleration tail, steps of 2.4 px, goes from 5.9 to 0.1 ms.\n\n**Fix**\n\n`For` keeps the ids it last placed and skips `replaceChildren` when the new ids are the same, in the same order. The patch is from the RayDE session.","files":null}]}